B.3 Modified L^{2} contraction and proof of the main theorem We now prove Theorem 3.5 using a discrete modified L^{2} argument. For f\in L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) , write f_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}f\,,\hskip 20.00003ptf_{\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f\,. Here f_{\mathsf{P}} and f_{\perp} are, respectively, the macroscopic and microscopic parts of f . Fix 0<\rho\leq 1/2 . The ordinary L^{2} energy does not by itself give the required one-step contraction. Indeed, since \mathsf{U} is unitary and \mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp} is a contraction, \displaystyle\|\mathsf{K}f\|^{2}-\|f\|^{2} \displaystyle=(1-\rho)^{2}\,\|\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}f_{\perp}\|^{2}-\|f_{\perp}\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq-\rho\,(2-\rho)\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\leq-\rho\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. (B.18) This estimate controls f_{\perp} but gives nothing when f=f_{\mathsf{P}} . Reflection moves part of f_{\mathsf{P}} into the microscopic subspace, but this transfer is not captured by the L^{2} norm, which is a standard difficulty in hypocoercivity. The modified L^{2} method addresses this difficulty by adding a corrector to the Lyapunov function. The corrector records the transfer from f_{\mathsf{P}} to f_{\perp} , allowing the contraction in ( B.18 ) to also control f_{\mathsf{P}} . This idea goes back to Dolbeault, Mouhot, and Schmeiser [ 14 ] ; here we follow the refinement of Fan, Li, and Lu [ 16 ] . To construct the corrector, we first isolate the part of f_{\perp} that couples to f_{\mathsf{P}} under reflection. Define f_{\mathsf{V}}\coloneqq\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp}\,. By ( B.16 ), \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}} , while the component of f_{\perp} orthogonal to \operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}) is annihilated by \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} . Since \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}} is an isometry, \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}}=\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp} is the orthogonal projection of f_{\perp} onto \operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}) , and hence \|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|\leq\|f_{\perp}\| . We work with f_{\mathsf{V}} , rather than with this projection itself, because f_{\mathsf{V}} and f_{\mathsf{P}} both belong to \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} and can therefore be paired in the corrector. With this notation, let \omega>0 be a weight to be chosen below and define the modified Lyapunov function \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\coloneqq\|f\|^{2}+\omega\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})\,, (B.19) where the corrector is \mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl(\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}-\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}\bigr)-\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr\rangle\,. (B.20) Here \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} is applied only on \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} , where \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\gamma_{\rm gap}I , so the corrector is well-defined. To motivate this choice, recall from ( B.7 ) that \mathsf{K} acts in two stages: it first maps f_{\mathsf{P}}+f_{\perp}\longmapsto f_{\mathsf{P}}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}f_{\perp} and then applies \mathsf{U} . For the moment, set \rho=0 and replace \mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp} by -I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}} . The error I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp} introduced by this replacement is controlled by Proposition B.2 . Denote the resulting idealized operator by \mathsf{K}_{\rm id}\coloneqq\mathsf{U}(\mathsf{P}-\mathsf{P}^{\perp})\,. Set g\coloneqq\mathsf{K}_{\rm id}f , and define the corresponding components by g_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}g\,,\hskip 20.00003ptg_{\mathsf{V}}\coloneqq\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}g\,. In terms of these components, \mathsf{K}_{\rm id} is represented by the operator-valued rotation matrix \begin{pmatrix}g_{\mathsf{P}}\\ g_{\mathsf{V}}\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&-\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\\ \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}&\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}f_{\mathsf{P}}\\ f_{\mathsf{V}}\end{pmatrix}\,. Indeed, the first component follows immediately from the block representation of \mathsf{U} and the polar decomposition ( B.16 ): \displaystyle g_{\mathsf{P}} \displaystyle=\mathsf{P}\mathsf{U}(f_{\mathsf{P}}-f_{\perp})=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}-\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp}=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}-\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp}=\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}-\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}}\,. For the second component, substitute \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} into \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} from ( B.5 ). Since \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} commutes with \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} , this gives \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\,. The ranges of \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp} and \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} lie in \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} , where \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} is bounded. Applying \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} to the displayed identity therefore gives \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} . Hence \displaystyle g_{\mathsf{V}} \displaystyle=\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}(f_{\mathsf{P}}-f_{\perp})=\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}(\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}-\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}f_{\perp})=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}+\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}f_{\perp}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}+\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}}\,. This proves the claimed matrix formula. Since \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}+\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I , the block matrix acts as a rotation on \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} . Under this rotation, direct substitution into ( B.20 ) gives \displaystyle\mathscr{C}(g_{\mathsf{P}},g_{\mathsf{V}})-\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}}) \displaystyle\hskip 10.00002pt=\mathscr{C}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}-\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}}\,,\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{P}}+\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f_{\mathsf{V}})-\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}}) \displaystyle\hskip 10.00002pt=-\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}\,. (B.21) For 0<\omega<\rho , combining the leading terms from ( B.18 ) and ( B.21 ), and using \|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|\leq\|f_{\perp}\| , gives \displaystyle-\rho\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}+\omega\,\bigl(-\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}\bigr)\leq-\omega\,\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}-(\rho-\omega)\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. Thus the leading terms control both components. This calculation makes explicit the role of the corrector in the modified L^{2} hypocoercivity argument; see also Fan, Li, and Lu [ 16 ] . We now turn from the idealized calculation to the contraction proof. The modified L^{2} argument has two steps: we first show that the Lyapunov function is equivalent to the squared L^{2} norm and then establish its one-step decay. Lemma B.3 (Equivalence with the squared L^{2} norm) . If 0<\omega\leq\gamma_{\rm gap} , then every f\in L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) satisfies \frac{1}{2}\,\|f\|^{2}\leq\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\leq\frac{3}{2}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. (B.22) Proof. The operators \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} and \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} commute, so \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} is self-adjoint on \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} . Moreover, I+(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1})^{2}=I+\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-2}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-2}\,, where the last identity uses \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}+\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I . By ( B.20 ), \displaystyle 2\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}}) \displaystyle=\Bigl\langle\begin{pmatrix}f_{\mathsf{P}}\\ f_{\mathsf{V}}\end{pmatrix}\,,\begin{pmatrix}I&-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\\ -\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}&-I\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}f_{\mathsf{P}}\\ f_{\mathsf{V}}\end{pmatrix}\Bigr\rangle\,. The square of the block operator in this display is \begin{pmatrix}I&-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\\ -\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}&-I\end{pmatrix}^{\!2}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-2}&0\\ 0&\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-2}\end{pmatrix}\,. Since the block operator is self-adjoint and \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\gamma_{\rm gap}I , \Bigl\|\begin{pmatrix}I&-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\\ -\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}&-I\end{pmatrix}\Bigr\|=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\|\leq\frac{1}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\,. Applying this operator norm bound to the preceding quadratic form gives |\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})|\leq\frac{1}{2\gamma_{\rm gap}}\,\bigl(\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}\bigr)\leq\frac{1}{2\gamma_{\rm gap}}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. (B.23) Here the second inequality uses \|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}\leq\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}=\|f\|^{2} . Finally, ( B.19 ), ( B.23 ), and \omega\leq\gamma_{\rm gap} imply \displaystyle\bigl|\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)-\|f\|^{2}\bigr| \displaystyle=\omega\,|\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})|\leq\frac{\omega}{2\gamma_{\rm gap}}\,\|f\|^{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,\|f\|^{2}\,.\qed (B.24) Lemma B.4 (One-step decay of the Lyapunov function) . There exist universal constants c_{\rm hyp},c_{0},c>0 such that the following holds. Set \Lambda_{\rho}\coloneqq\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})+\Bigl(\frac{\rho}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\Bigr)^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\omega\coloneqq\frac{c_{\rm hyp}\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\,. (B.25) If \beta\eta\leq c_{0} , then every f\in L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) satisfies \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}f)\leq\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\,. (B.26) Proof. Take c_{0}\leq 1/16 . Then \gamma_{\rm gap}\leq 2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}\leq 1/2 , and consequently \Lambda_{\rho}\geq\log 2 . We compare the actual update with the idealized operator analyzed above. Let g\coloneqq\mathsf{K}_{\rm id}f , g_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}g , and g_{\mathsf{V}}\coloneqq\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}g . By the definitions of \mathsf{K} and \mathsf{K}_{\rm id} , \displaystyle\mathsf{K}f-\mathsf{K}_{\rm id}f \displaystyle=\mathsf{U}\bigl[I+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]f_{\perp} \displaystyle=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\rho f_{\perp}+(1-\rho)(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})f_{\perp}\bigr]\,. Using \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} and \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} , the two components of this difference are \displaystyle\mathsf{P}(\mathsf{K}f-\mathsf{K}_{\rm id}f) \displaystyle=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\bigl[I+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]f_{\perp}\,, \displaystyle\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}(\mathsf{K}f-\mathsf{K}_{\rm id}f) \displaystyle=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\bigl[I+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]f_{\perp}\,. The same vector therefore determines both components of the error. Denote it by r_{\rho}\coloneqq\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\bigl[I+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]f_{\perp}=\rho f_{\mathsf{V}}+(1-\rho)r\,,\hskip 20.00003ptr\coloneqq\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})f_{\perp}\,. Both r and r_{\rho} belong to \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} . In r_{\rho} , the first term comes from refreshment, while the second measures the departure of the half turn from -I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}} . Consequently, (\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{P}}=g_{\mathsf{P}}+\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt(\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{V}}=g_{\mathsf{V}}-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\,. (B.27) Substituting ( B.27 ) into the definition ( B.20 ) of the corrector \mathscr{C} and expanding gives \displaystyle\mathscr{C}\bigl((\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{P}},(\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr)-\mathscr{C}(g_{\mathsf{P}},g_{\mathsf{V}}) \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt=\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{V}},\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl(\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}-\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}\bigr) \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt\hskip 20.00003pt+{}\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle-\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho},g_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr\rangle+\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2} \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt=\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\frac{1}{2}\,\bigl(\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}+\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}\bigr)\,. In the last equality, the two terms containing g_{\mathsf{V}} cancel. For the terms linear in g_{\mathsf{P}} , the commutation of \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} with \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} and the identity \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}+\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}=I give \displaystyle\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\bigl\langle\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt=\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},(\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}+\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle=\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle\,. The same identity reduces the quadratic terms to \frac{1}{2}\,\bigl(\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}+\|\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}r_{\rho}\|^{2}\bigr)=\frac{1}{2}\,\|r_{\rho}\|^{2}\,. Consequently, combined with ( B.21 ), \displaystyle\mathscr{C}\bigl((\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{P}},(\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr)-\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}}) \displaystyle\hskip 10.00002pt=\bigl[\mathscr{C}\bigl((\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{P}},(\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr)-\mathscr{C}(g_{\mathsf{P}},g_{\mathsf{V}})\bigr]+\bigl[\mathscr{C}(g_{\mathsf{P}},g_{\mathsf{V}})-\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})\bigr] \displaystyle\hskip 10.00002pt=-\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}+\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle+\frac{1}{2}\,\|r_{\rho}\|^{2}\,. (B.28) Thus, it remains to estimate r_{\rho} . Let \mathsf{L}\coloneqq(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\,. Since r=\mathsf{L}^{*}f_{\perp} , applying ( B.17 ) to \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}v and then taking adjoints gives \displaystyle\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r\|^{2}=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})f_{\perp}\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. (B.29) Together with \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\succeq\gamma_{\rm gap}I and \|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|\leq\|f_{\perp}\| , this gives \displaystyle\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r_{\rho}\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq 2\rho^{2}\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}+2(1-\rho)^{2}\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r\|^{2}\leq C\Lambda_{\rho}\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. (B.30) Moreover, since \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*} and \mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp} are contractions, \|r_{\rho}\|\leq\rho\,\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|+(1-\rho)\,\|r\|\leq 2\,\|f_{\perp}\| . The rotation formula for \mathsf{K}_{\rm id} gives \|g_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}\leq\|g_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|g_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}=\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2} . Hence, Young’s inequality and ( B.30 ) give \displaystyle\bigl|\bigl\langle g_{\mathsf{P}},\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r_{\rho}\bigr\rangle\bigr| \displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{4}\,\|g_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}r_{\rho}\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{4}\,\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\frac{1}{4}\,\|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|^{2}+C\Lambda_{\rho}\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. Inserting these estimates into ( B.28 ), and using \|f_{\mathsf{V}}\|\leq\|f_{\perp}\| and \Lambda_{\rho}\geq\log 2 , gives \displaystyle\mathscr{C}\bigl((\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{P}},(\mathsf{K}f)_{\mathsf{V}}\bigr)-\mathscr{C}(f_{\mathsf{P}},f_{\mathsf{V}})\leq-\frac{1}{2}\,\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+C\Lambda_{\rho}\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. (B.31) Finally, ( B.18 ) and ( B.31 ) give \displaystyle\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}f)-\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f) \displaystyle\leq-\rho\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}+\frac{c_{\rm hyp}\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\,\Bigl(-\frac{1}{2}\,\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+C\Lambda_{\rho}\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\Bigr) \displaystyle=-\frac{c_{\rm hyp}\rho}{2\Lambda_{\rho}}\,\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}-(1-Cc_{\rm hyp})\,\rho\,\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\,. Choose c_{\rm hyp}\leq 1 sufficiently small so that Cc_{\rm hyp}\leq 1/2 . Since \Lambda_{\rho}\geq\log 2 , the last display is then bounded above by -\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\,\bigl(\|f_{\mathsf{P}}\|^{2}+\|f_{\perp}\|^{2}\bigr)=-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. It remains to compare the last norm with the Lyapunov function. The elementary inequality x/(a+x^{2})\leq 1/(2\sqrt{a}) , for a>0 and x\geq 0 , gives \frac{\omega}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}=c_{\rm hyp}\,\frac{\rho/\gamma_{\rm gap}}{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})+(\rho/\gamma_{\rm gap})^{2}}\leq\frac{c_{\rm hyp}}{2\sqrt{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})}}\leq\frac{c_{\rm hyp}}{2\sqrt{\log 2}}\leq 1\,. Thus \omega\leq\gamma_{\rm gap} , and the upper bound in Lemma B.3 gives \|f\|^{2}\geq\frac{2}{3}\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\,. Consequently, \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}f)\leq\Bigl(1-\frac{2c\rho}{3\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\,, which is ( B.26 ) after renaming the universal constant. ∎ Combining Lemmas B.3 and B.4 , we can now prove the L^{2} contraction in Theorem 3.5 . Proof of Theorem 3.5 . We first turn the one-step Lyapunov decay into a fixed L^{2} contraction over a block of N steps, and then apply this contraction to densities. Take c_{0} from Lemma B.4 and choose a fixed universal \rho_{\star}\in(0,1/2] . The one-step estimate requires \rho\leq 1/2 . Since \alpha\eta\leq\beta\eta\leq 1 and x\log(e/x)\leq 1 for 0<x\leq 1 , the prescribed refreshment probability satisfies 0<\rho=\rho_{\star}\sqrt{\alpha\eta\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\leq\rho_{\star}\leq\frac{1}{2}\,. The decay rate in Lemma B.4 is \rho/\Lambda_{\rho} , so a fixed contraction is obtained by taking N proportional to \Lambda_{\rho}/\rho . We first bound this ratio. Recall that \gamma_{\rm gap}\asymp\sqrt{\alpha\eta} and \log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\asymp\log(e/(\alpha\eta)) . Hence \Bigl(\frac{\rho}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\Bigr)^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\rho\geq c\gamma_{\rm gap}\sqrt{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})}\,. It follows from the definition of \Lambda_{\rho} that \displaystyle\frac{\Lambda_{\rho}}{\rho} \displaystyle\leq C\,\frac{\sqrt{\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})}}{\gamma_{\rm gap}}\leq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,. Choose a sufficiently large universal constant C_{1} and consider N\geq C_{1}\,\frac{\Lambda_{\rho}}{\rho}\,. (B.32) For f\in L_{0}^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) , take \omega as in ( B.25 ). Iterating ( B.26 ) over this block gives \mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}^{N}f)\leq\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\,. Applying the lower bound in ( B.22 ) to \mathsf{K}^{N}f and the upper bound to f , we obtain \displaystyle\|\mathsf{K}^{N}f\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq 2\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(\mathsf{K}^{N}f)\leq 2\,\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\leq 3\,\Bigl(1-\frac{c\rho}{\Lambda_{\rho}}\Bigr)^{N}\,\|f\|^{2}\leq 3e^{-cC_{1}}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. The last step uses 1-x\leq e^{-x} and N\geq C_{1}\Lambda_{\rho}/\rho . Taking C_{1} sufficiently large makes 3e^{-cC_{1}}\leq e^{-1} . Thus, \|\mathsf{K}^{N}f\|^{2}\leq e^{-1}\,\|f\|^{2}\,. (B.33) Iterating by blocks, the L^{2} contraction ( B.33 ) is equivalently, by ( D.5 ) and the adjoint remark following it, the \chi^{2} -estimate \chi^{2}\bigl(\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN}\|\pi_{\eta}\bigr)\leq e^{-J}\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\,, which proves ( 3.14 ). The X -marginal of \pi_{\eta} is \mu , and total variation cannot increase under projection. Moreover, Cauchy–Schwarz gives \operatorname{TV}(P,Q)\leq\tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{\chi^{2}(P\|Q)} , which gives ( 3.15 ). ∎